# Shell environment — Windows + Git Bash + shared venv

## What this rule guarantees

The shared Python venv at C:\Users\asingh\envs\shared-3.10 must be
active for any Python invocation. Cursor on Windows defaults to
PowerShell for the agent's Shell tool, which does NOT inherit the
venv or config/.env unless explicitly configured.

## Required Cursor settings (verify at project start)

Your user settings.json must contain BOTH:

    "terminal.integrated.defaultProfile.windows": "Git Bash",
    "terminal.integrated.automationProfile.windows": {
        "path": "C:\\Program Files\\Git\\bin\\bash.exe",
        "args": ["-l", "-i"]
    }

The defaultProfile controls the interactive terminal panel. The
automationProfile controls what the agent Shell tool spawns. They
are SEPARATE settings — setting only defaultProfile leaves the
agent running PowerShell. This is the most common Windows
misconfiguration that breaks all subsequent agent work.

The `-l -i` args matter: -l (login shell) sources .bash_profile,
which sources .bashrc; -i (interactive) also triggers .bashrc.
Without these, the venv won't auto-activate and config/.env won't
load.

## Environment verification at session start

Before running any data pull or analysis, verify the shell with:

    echo "shell=$0" && which python && \
      python -c "import os; print('FRED_KEY_OK=', \
      bool(os.environ.get('FRED_API_KEY')))" && \
      python -c "import os; print('MSGRAPH_OK=', \
      bool(os.environ.get('AS_MSGRAPH_CLIENT_SECRET')))"

Expected output:
shell=/usr/bin/bash
/c/Users/asingh/envs/shared-3.10/Scripts/python
FRED_KEY_OK= True
MSGRAPH_OK= True

If shell shows anything PowerShell-related, STOP and tell Aman to
update Cursor's automationProfile setting. Do NOT try to fix shell
config from inside the agent loop. The fix is: 1. Edit user settings.json (Ctrl+, → JSON view) 2. Add the automationProfile block above 3. Developer: Reload Window 4. Re-verify

If python doesn't resolve to /c/Users/asingh/envs/shared-3.10/Scripts/python,
the .bashrc venv auto-activation isn't running.

If FRED_KEY_OK or MSGRAPH_OK is False, config/.env isn't being
sourced from .bashrc. Both are user-level configuration issues.

## Path conventions in Git Bash

Git Bash translates /c/Users/... to C:\Users\... when passing as
COMMAND ARGUMENTS, but NOT when paths appear inside string literals
sent to `python -c`. Inside Python -c strings, use Windows-style
paths with forward slashes:

    # WRONG — Python sees the literal /c/Users/... which isn't valid
    python -c "import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/c/Users/asingh/foo')"

    # RIGHT — Windows path with forward slashes (Python accepts both)
    python -c "import sys; sys.path.insert(0, 'C:/Users/asingh/foo')"

    # ALSO RIGHT — use cygpath to convert at command time
    python -c "import sys; sys.path.insert(0, r'$(cygpath -m /c/Users/asingh/foo)')"

The cleanest pattern: cd into the relevant directory and use
relative paths in -c strings.

## Anti-patterns

- DO NOT translate bash commands to PowerShell syntax to work around
  a PowerShell-only shell. The fix is to switch the shell.
- DO NOT pip install --upgrade to "fix" import issues. Verify which
  Python is running first.
- DO NOT assume the agent's automation shell inherits the
  interactive terminal's environment. They're separate.
- DO NOT silently re-run things in PowerShell because Get-ChildItem
  errors look like bash errors. The first sign that && or other
  bash tokens fail = PowerShell, fix the profile.
